---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../../components/Footer.astro";

const title = "Plumbing & HVAC Revenue Leak Discovery | Google LSA Help";
const description =
  "Paying for Google LSA leads that never become jobs? A fixed-price investigation of your leads, calls, bookings, and job economics for plumbing and HVAC.";
const canonical =
  "https://matteoarellano.com/services/plumbing-hvac-revenue-leak-discovery/";

// Replace this with the exact Cal.com event URL already used on your website.
const CALENDAR_URL =
  "https://cal.com/matteo-arellano-seo-visibility/20-min-google-ads-and-local-service-ads-lsa-revenue-leak";

const WHATSAPP_URL =
  "https://wa.me/525574136001?text=Hi%20Matteo%2C%20I%20run%20a%20plumbing%20or%20HVAC%20company.%20Our%20Google%20leads%20are%20not%20turning%20into%20enough%20profitable%20jobs.";

const faqItems = [
  {
    question:
      "Is Google Local Services Ads even worth it for plumbers and HVAC companies?",
    answer: `For many companies, yes, because LSA sits at the top of the search results and charges per lead rather than per click. But whether it is worth it depends on your market, answer rate, booking process, job mix, and margins. The honest way to answer the question is to calculate what your LSA leads cost per completed job and compare that with what those jobs are worth to your company.`,
  },
  {
    question: "What is a good cost per lead?",
    answer: `There is no universal number. A $75 lead can be cheap when it regularly becomes a high-value replacement and expensive when it becomes an unbooked call about a small repair. I calculate cost per qualified lead, per booked appointment, and per completed job before deciding whether your lead price is actually the problem.`,
  },
  {
    question: "How much should I budget for LSA?",
    answer: `Enough for the ad to stay active during the services and hours your company can actually cover, and no more than your call handling can absorb. Raising the budget while calls go unanswered simply buys more missed opportunities. Budget is reviewed after lead quality, responsiveness, and booking performance are understood.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Can missed calls really hurt my LSA performance?",
    answer: `Yes, in two ways. Google tracks responsiveness and says that answering calls can affect ranking and the ability to receive leads. Separately, an urgent customer who reaches voicemail usually calls the next company. Missed calls are one of the most common and most fixable revenue leaks.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Why am I getting charged for wrong or irrelevant leads?",
    answer: `Google moved from manual lead disputes to automated lead credits in 2024. Some invalid calls receive credits, but owners still report paying for wrong-service, out-of-area, duplicate, and existing-customer calls. The practical response is to review job types, service areas, hours, profile consistency, and lead feedback so fewer bad contacts arrive and the account receives clearer signals.`,
  },
  {
    question: "I am just setting up LSA. Can you help before I spend anything?",
    answer: `Yes. Verification, documents, job-type selection, service areas, schedules, phone coverage, and tracking all influence what happens once the account starts spending. For a new account, the work focuses on setup quality, market conditions, call handling, and a simple way to measure leads through to completed jobs.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need an AI receptionist or an answering service?",
    answer: `Possibly, but the evidence should decide. A company missing calls only during lunch may need call forwarding and a strict callback rule. A company losing high-value emergency calls every night may need reliable after-hours coverage. I review when calls are being lost and what those calls are worth before recommending a vendor or tool.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Will fixing my website improve my LSA results?",
    answer: `Not directly. LSA ranking is driven by factors such as proximity, reviews, verification, relevance, and responsiveness rather than website quality. However, customers may still look up your company before calling, so a weak or inconsistent website can reduce trust and bookings. I review the website only where it affects the wider customer journey.`,
  },
  {
    question: "What is the difference between LSA and regular Google Ads?",
    answer: `They are separate products. Local Services Ads charge per lead, require verification, and appear in a dedicated local-services format. Standard Google Search Ads usually charge per click and give much more control over keywords, targeting, and landing pages. Many companies use both, but the correct mix depends on the services and customers they want.`,
  },
  {
    question: "What data and access do you need, and is it safe to share?",
    answer: `Viewer-level access to your LSA account and Google Business Profile is enough to begin. I also need recent lead records, call logs and recordings where available, and basic booking and job-value information. I do not need banking details, and nothing is changed without your approval. Exports and screen shares can also be used when direct access is not preferred.`,
  },
  {
    question:
      "My office and some of my technicians work in Spanish. Does that help?",
    answer: `Yes. I work in English and Spanish, so I can review Spanish-language calls directly, examine how customers are qualified, and help create bilingual call scripts and service descriptions. This can be especially useful when the company operates bilingually but the marketing and phone process were built only in English.`,
  },
  {
    question: "What happens after the one-time discovery?",
    answer: `The Revenue Leak Discovery stands on its own. You receive the explanation, the prioritized plan, help implementing the first approved changes, and fourteen days for follow-up questions. Monthly Follow-Through is optional and available only when the first engagement was useful to you.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Do you guarantee results?",
    answer: `No. Lead volume and cost depend on Google, competitors, seasonality, demand, and your local market. What I commit to is a defined investigation, a clear written explanation, a prioritized plan, and practical help implementing the findings. Anyone guaranteeing a specific LSA ranking, lead volume, or revenue outcome should be treated carefully.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Are you affiliated with Google?",
    answer: `No. This is an independent consulting service. I am not employed by, partnered with, or endorsed by Google, and I do not receive commissions from software vendors, answering services, or agencies that may be discussed as possible solutions.`,
  },
];

const serviceSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  name: "Plumbing and HVAC Revenue Leak Discovery",
  description,
  serviceType:
    "Google Local Services Ads, call handling, booking, and job-economics investigation for plumbing and HVAC companies",
  provider: {
    "@type": "Person",
    name: "Matteo Arellano",
    url: "https://matteoarellano.com/",
    email: "matteo.visibility@gmail.com",
    knowsLanguage: ["English", "Spanish", "Catalan", "Dutch", "Norwegian"],
  },
  areaServed: {
    "@type": "Country",
    name: "United States",
  },
  offers: [
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      name: "Revenue Leak Discovery",
      price: "599",
      priceCurrency: "USD",
      description:
        "A fixed-price, one-time investigation of Google Local Services Ads, lead quality, call response, booking performance, dispatch, and job economics.",
    },
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      name: "Monthly Follow-Through",
      price: "750",
      priceCurrency: "USD",
      description:
        "Optional month-to-month support with performance reviews, implementation guidance, and one or two working sessions per month.",
    },
  ],
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      "@type": "Answer",
      text: item.answer,
    },
  })),
};

const personSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  name: "Matteo Arellano",
  url: "https://matteoarellano.com/",
  email: "matteo.visibility@gmail.com",
  jobTitle: "Growth Analytics and Technical SEO Consultant",
  knowsAbout: [
    "Google Local Services Ads",
    "Google Ads",
    "Conversion rate optimization",
    "GA4",
    "BigQuery",
    "Python",
    "Lead quality analysis",
    "Call and booking analysis",
    "Website performance",
    "Financial analysis",
  ],
  knowsLanguage: ["English", "Spanish", "Catalan", "Dutch", "Norwegian"],
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 1,
      name: "Home",
      item: "https://matteoarellano.com/",
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 2,
      name: "Services",
      item: "https://matteoarellano.com/services/",
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 3,
      name: "Plumbing & HVAC Revenue Leak Discovery",
      item: canonical,
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  lang="en"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(personSchema)} />
  <script
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)}
  />

  <Header />

  <main class="revenue-page commercial-page">
    <section class="revenue-hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <div class="commercial-container revenue-hero-grid">
        <div class="revenue-hero-copy">
          <span class="revenue-kicker">
            For owners and managers of US plumbing and HVAC companies
          </span>

          <h1 id="page-title" class="revenue-hero-title">
            You're probably not short on leads.
            <span>You're losing them between the phone and the invoice.</span>
          </h1>

          <p class="revenue-hero-lede">
            Google Local Services Ads can send you calls all week and your
            company can still end the month with thin margins. Wrong-service
            calls you got charged for. Calls that rang out while your
            technicians were on jobs. Customers who never got a callback after 6
            pm. Bookings that turned into small repairs two towns over. I
            investigate the full path from Google to the completed job and show
            you where the revenue is leaking.
          </p>

          <p class="revenue-hero-note">
            One fixed price. A clear explanation of the problem, a prioritized
            plan, and help putting the first fixes in place. No retainer, no
            software pitch, and no dashboard that stops at cost per lead.
          </p>

          <div class="commercial-actions">
            <a class="commercial-primary" href="#book-call">
              Book a 20-minute fit call
            </a>
            <a
              class="commercial-secondary"
              href={WHATSAPP_URL}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Message me on WhatsApp
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="trust-row" aria-label="Service highlights">
            <span class="trust-pill">$599 fixed price, one time</span>
            <span class="trust-pill">Independent, no software commissions</span>
            <span class="trust-pill">English and Spanish support</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <aside class="revenue-hero-panel" aria-label="Revenue leak path">
          <p class="revenue-panel-label">Where revenue leaks</p>
          <ol class="revenue-flow">
            <li>
              <span class="revenue-flow-number">01</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Google visibility</strong>
                <small>Budget caps, account issues, or stronger profiles</small>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="revenue-flow-number">02</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Call or message</strong>
                <small>Wrong trade, wrong area, or existing customer</small>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="revenue-flow-number">03</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Response</strong>
                <small>Missed call, slow callback, or after-hours gap</small>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="revenue-flow-number">04</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Qualification and booking</strong>
                <small>Weak questions or no firm appointment</small>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="revenue-flow-number">05</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Dispatch and completion</strong>
                <small>Drive time, low-value work, or lost estimates</small>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="revenue-flow-number">06</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Revenue and profit</strong>
                <small>The lead never gets connected to the invoice</small>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      class="commercial-section commercial-soft"
      aria-labelledby="journey-heading"
    >
      <div class="commercial-container">
        <div class="commercial-section-header">
          <p class="commercial-eyebrow">The full revenue journey</p>
          <h2 id="journey-heading">
            The dashboard only shows the beginning of the story.
          </h2>
          <p>
            Every dollar you collect from Google travels through six stages.
            Money can leak at any of them, and the LSA dashboard only gives you
            a partial view of the first two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="commercial-card-grid">
          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">01 · Google visibility</span>
            <h3>Your phone may never get the chance to ring</h3>
            <p>
              Budgets can cap out mid-week. License or insurance documents can
              expire. A profile edit can trigger a review. Competitors with
              stronger reviews or faster response can take the limited ad
              positions available in your market.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">02 · Call or message</span>
            <h3>The lead may be worth nothing</h3>
            <p>
              A homeowner may request a trade you do not provide. A residential
              caller may reach a commercial-only company. The customer may live
              outside the area where the job is profitable. The call could also
              be spam, a job seeker, or an existing customer who tapped the ad.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">03 · Response</span>
            <h3>One missed call can cost you twice</h3>
            <p>
              A qualified customer calls while everyone is on a job. The call
              rings out, the customer calls the next provider, and Google logs
              the missed contact against your responsiveness. You lose the job
              today and may receive fewer opportunities later.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">04 · Qualification and booking</span>
            <h3>A real customer still needs a firm next step</h3>
            <p>
              The phone gets answered, but the right questions are not asked, no
              appointment window is offered, or the caller is told someone will
              call back. The lead was real and paid for, yet the job still goes
              to the company that booked it on the first conversation.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">05 · Dispatch and completion</span>
            <h3>The schedule can fill with the wrong work</h3>
            <p>
              The booking may involve two hours of driving, a low-value repair
              during peak season, or an estimate that receives no follow-up.
              Revenue looks acceptable until technician time and capacity are
              taken into account.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">06 · Revenue and profit</span>
            <h3>The completed job must connect back to the lead</h3>
            <p>
              Without that link, you cannot identify which services, locations,
              schedules, or lead types produce profitable work. The next budget
              gets allocated using the same incomplete numbers as the last one.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="commercial-section" aria-labelledby="recognition-heading">
      <div class="commercial-container">
        <div class="commercial-section-header">
          <p class="commercial-eyebrow">Does this sound familiar?</p>
          <h2 id="recognition-heading">
            These are the situations plumbing and HVAC owners describe.
          </h2>
          <p>
            They are not abstract marketing problems. They are calls, jobs,
            schedules, and invoices that did not turn out the way the dashboard
            suggested they would.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="commercial-proof">
          <div class="revenue-offer-columns">
            <ul class="revenue-check-list">
              <li>
                You got charged for someone asking for a completely different
                trade and could only rate the lead and hope for a credit.
              </li>
              <li>
                Existing customers search your company name, tap the LSA ad, and
                become paid leads even though they were already yours.
              </li>
              <li>
                The dashboard estimated a lead volume your account has never
                come close to reaching.
              </li>
              <li>
                You want commercial work, but residential faucet and drip calls
                keep consuming money and office time.
              </li>
              <li>
                Your best calls arrive at lunch, in the evening, or on weekends
                when nobody can respond within minutes.
              </li>
              <li>
                The ads run longer than your phone is genuinely covered, so
                after-hours customers quietly go to the company that answered.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <ul class="revenue-check-list">
              <li>
                Ads stopped because of an expired document, a billing problem,
                or an account review nobody noticed quickly.
              </li>
              <li>
                A large competitor with thousands of reviews sits above you and
                nobody can explain which factor is actually responsible.
              </li>
              <li>
                Cost per lead rises every season, but nobody knows what a lead
                can sustainably cost because the booking rate is unclear.
              </li>
              <li>
                Your agency reports impressions and leads but cannot say how
                many became completed jobs.
              </li>
              <li>
                Performance changes with the season and nobody can separate
                market demand from account or operational problems.
              </li>
              <li>
                The office blames lead quality, technicians blame scheduling,
                the agency blames Google, and you are still signing the checks.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      class="commercial-section revenue-economics"
      aria-labelledby="economics-heading"
    >
      <div class="commercial-container revenue-economics-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="commercial-eyebrow">Why the dashboard is incomplete</p>
          <h2 id="economics-heading">
            Cost per lead is where the dashboard stops. It is where your losses
            start.
          </h2>
          <p>
            The dashboard tells you how many leads arrived and what they cost.
            It cannot tell you how many were real opportunities, how many got
            answered, how many booked, how many jobs were completed, or what
            those jobs were worth. Those numbers live in your phone system,
            schedule, CRM, and invoices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div
          class="revenue-equation"
          aria-label="Cost per completed job calculation"
        >
          <div class="revenue-equation-line">
            <span>$70</span>
            <span>÷</span>
            <span>(0.50 × 0.60 × 0.80)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="revenue-equation-result">≈ $292 per completed job</div>
          <p>
            Assume half the leads are qualified, 60% of qualified callers book,
            and 80% of bookings become completed jobs. That $292 may work for a
            replacement, repipe, sewer job, or installation. It can be brutal
            for a small repair with thin margins and long drive time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Improve the booking rate from 60% to 75% and the same $70 lead costs
            about $233 per completed job, without increasing the ad budget.
            Until the weak number in the chain is known, every fix is a guess.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      class="commercial-section commercial-soft"
      aria-labelledby="discovery-heading"
    >
      <div class="commercial-container revenue-offers">
        <div class="revenue-offer-card is-primary">
          <div class="revenue-offer-header">
            <div>
              <p class="commercial-eyebrow">One-time engagement</p>
              <h2 id="discovery-heading">Revenue Leak Discovery</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="revenue-price">
              <strong>$599</strong>
              <span>fixed price · one time</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="revenue-offer-intro">
            A focused investigation into everything that happens between Google
            showing your company and your company collecting revenue from a
            completed job. I go through the account, the leads, the calls, the
            booking process, and the available job economics until the problem
            has a clear explanation you can act on.
          </p>

          <div class="revenue-offer-columns">
            <div>
              <h3>What I investigate</h3>
              <ul class="revenue-check-list">
                <li>
                  Verification, services, job types, service areas, hours, ad
                  schedule, bidding, budget pacing, and local competitors
                </li>
                <li>
                  Google Business Profile reviews, photos, categories, and
                  consistency where they affect LSA and customer trust
                </li>
                <li>
                  Real opportunities versus wrong-service, wrong-area, spam,
                  duplicate, job-seeker, and existing-customer leads
                </li>
                <li>
                  Answered and missed calls, callback speed, after-hours
                  coverage, messages, and call routing
                </li>
                <li>
                  Qualification questions, appointment booking, dispatch, drive
                  time, technician capacity, and estimate follow-up
                </li>
                <li>
                  Cost per qualified lead, booked appointment, and completed
                  job, plus revenue and estimated margin where data allows
                </li>
                <li>
                  Tracking gaps between Google, the phone system, scheduling or
                  CRM tools, and completed jobs
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3>What you receive</h3>
              <ul class="revenue-check-list">
                <li>
                  A kickoff call about your market, capacity, target services,
                  and the work you no longer want to pay for
                </li>
                <li>
                  A review of up to 90 days of available lead, call, booking,
                  and job data
                </li>
                <li>
                  A plain-English written explanation separating account,
                  operational, and market problems
                </li>
                <li>
                  A prioritized plan ordered by financial impact, cost, and
                  effort
                </li>
                <li>
                  A clear distinction between free fixes, process changes, and
                  recommendations that may justify additional spending
                </li>
                <li>
                  A results meeting with you and the people who handle your
                  leads
                </li>
                <li>
                  Help implementing the first approved changes and fourteen days
                  for follow-up questions
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="revenue-scope-note">
            <strong>What I need from you:</strong>
            viewer access to LSA and the Google Business Profile, recent lead and
            call records, available call recordings, basic booking and job information,
            and time with the owner and whoever answers the phone. If this data does
            not exist, that is itself a useful finding.
          </div>

          <div class="revenue-offer-footer">
            <div>
              <strong>Typical delivery:</strong>
              <span>
                7 to 10 business days after the required access and data are
                available.
              </span>
            </div>
            <a class="commercial-primary" href="#book-call">
              Discuss the Revenue Leak Discovery
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="revenue-offer-card">
          <div class="revenue-offer-header">
            <div>
              <p class="commercial-eyebrow">Optional continued support</p>
              <h2 id="monthly-heading">Monthly Follow-Through</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="revenue-price">
              <strong>$750</strong>
              <span>fixed price · per month</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="revenue-offer-intro">
            Some owners take the plan and run with it internally. Others want
            someone staying on the numbers while answer rates drift, seasons
            change, competitors adjust, and Google changes the product. This is
            for the second group, and only when the discovery itself proved
            useful.
          </p>

          <div class="revenue-monthly-grid">
            <div class="revenue-monthly-item">
              <span>01</span>
              <h3>Monthly commercial review</h3>
              <p>
                Lead volume, lead quality, answer rate, bookings, completed
                jobs, and the commercial results your available data can
                support.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="revenue-monthly-item">
              <span>02</span>
              <h3>One or two working sessions</h3>
              <p>
                One scheduled results call, plus a second implementation or
                problem-solving session when the work requires it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="revenue-monthly-item">
              <span>03</span>
              <h3>Practical follow-through</h3>
              <p>
                Account adjustments, lead classification, call-flow changes,
                tracking improvements, and coordination with phone, scheduling,
                or answering-service vendors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="revenue-monthly-item">
              <span>04</span>
              <h3>Month-to-month support</h3>
              <p>
                No locked-in management contract. You can stop at any time, your
                accounts remain yours, and meaningful changes are explained
                first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="revenue-scope-note">
            <strong>Costs that stay separate:</strong>
            Google advertising spend, answering-service or call-center fees, CRM and
            software subscriptions, and major website development are always separate
            and discussed before anything is recommended.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="commercial-section" aria-labelledby="solutions-heading">
      <div class="commercial-container">
        <div class="commercial-section-header">
          <p class="commercial-eyebrow">Possible findings and solutions</p>
          <h2 id="solutions-heading">
            Different leaks need different fixes. That is why the investigation
            comes first.
          </h2>
          <p>
            Two companies can report the same symptom and have opposite
            problems. The solution should follow the evidence rather than the
            tool a vendor wants to sell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="commercial-card-grid">
          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">Missed calls</span>
            <h3>Match coverage to the value and timing of the calls</h3>
            <p>
              Possible fixes include adjusting the ad schedule, simple call
              forwarding, instant missed-call texts, a strict callback rule, a
              human answering service, or an AI receptionist when the economics
              support it.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">Wrong leads</span>
            <h3>Correct the signals before accepting the waste</h3>
            <p>
              Job types, service areas, hours, and profile consistency may need
              correction. Lead feedback should be used consistently. In other
              cases, some noise is unavoidable and must be included in the real
              cost per completed job.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">Low visibility</span>
            <h3>Identify the actual ranking constraint</h3>
            <p>
              Bids and budget are only two suspects. Review strength,
              responsiveness, verification, schedule settings, document status,
              demand, and stronger competitors may be the real cause.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">High acquisition cost</span>
            <h3>Find the weak conversion point</h3>
            <p>
              The leak may sit in qualification, booking, completion, estimate
              follow-up, service mix, or geographic mix. Spending more will not
              solve a weak handoff after the lead arrives.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">Verification problems</span>
            <h3>Resolve the administrative block and prevent a repeat</h3>
            <p>
              Expired licenses, insurance documents, profile mismatches, billing
              problems, and account reviews can stop ads quietly. The fix may be
              simple administration plus a recurring check.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">Poor booking rate</span>
            <h3>Give the person answering the phone a workable process</h3>
            <p>
              A short qualification checklist, authority to book, concrete time
              windows, clearer scripts, or overflow routing may be more valuable
              than changing anything inside Google.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">Unprofitable areas</span>
            <h3>Stop treating all booked jobs as equally valuable</h3>
            <p>
              Certain zip codes can look acceptable as revenue and become poor
              business after technician time and drive distance. Service areas,
              minimum job values, or the promoted service mix may need to
              change.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="commercial-card">
            <span class="tag">Weak tracking</span>
            <h3>Start with the simplest system that answers the question</h3>
            <p>
              A consistent lead-source field, a shared sheet, or data already
              available in the phone and scheduling systems may be enough.
              Larger tools should come later, when the volume and need justify
              them.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

